From 90e2415a5e41b4412ca1d458e2d6aa131c9cef83 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:09:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio: writer thread and a one-second capture ring on the
 record path

The reader thread wrote audio_capture.raw on the recording medium itself,
behind a 100 ms ALSA buffer. Cinemate found the failure this invites
(ffb61fd, 5021ba3): under 4K DNG pressure an exFAT write() blocks for
hundreds of milliseconds, the capture thread cannot read the ring, the
ring overruns and samples are gone. Core's discontinuity padding keeps the
WAV the right length, so the loss shows as a silent hole and as a lower
frames-per-second figure in the whole-take drift measurement.

- AudioRecordingSession: the reader hands blocks (and gap zeros) to a RAM
  queue; a writer thread drains them into the raw file. The reader never
  touches the medium. Write failures latch as before; a backlog above 16 MB
  logs once; stop() drains the writer, or lets it discard when the medium is
  gone, and logs the take's peak backlog.
- TimestampedAudioCapture::open() sets the hardware parameters by hand:
  buffer and period are independent, so the record path gets a 1 s ring
  (APERTAR_AUDIO_BUFFER_MS, 100..4000) with a 10 ms period; the negotiated
  sizes are logged. snd_pcm_set_params() tied the period to buffer/4.

Host-checked: AudioRecordingSession.cpp passes g++ -fsyntax-only;
TimestampedAudioCapture.cpp needs alsa/asoundlib.h and is not compiled here.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01T93jucKMhWYnQM1yyFVkbH
